# Phase 2 smoke gate for the lighting-composition change.
#
# The lighting HLSL is compiled at RUNTIME (load_lighting_shader_source reads
# data/shaders/lighting/src from the install), so NOTHING in the C++ build proves
# sprite.frag.hlsl even parses. Launching the game is the only compile gate there is.
#
# It also answers the main-menu question directly. The backdrop used to be a
# fullscreen quad with tint (0,0,0.3) relying on `max(tint, gpu_total)` to let the
# decorative menu emitter glow through. It is now mode gpu_lit with a white tint and
# the blue base in the `flash` lane, composited as
# `radiance * (1 - 0.3) + (0,0,0.3)` -- so the glow must still be there, ~30%
# dimmer, over the same blue.
#
# Paired same-state captures: this menu animates its own emitter, so without a null
# there is no way to separate a real difference from frame-to-frame shimmer.
launch --dont-debugmsg
waitlog "opened document data/gui/mainmenu.rml" timeout=240000
focus
settle timeout=15000

# The decorative emitter sits at tile (8.5, 4.5) by default, i.e. up and to the left,
# so a glow corner and a far corner are all that is needed to show it still lights the
# backdrop instead of having been replaced by a flat blue.
rect glow 0,0,0.25,0.25
rect far  0.75,0.75,0.25,0.25

shot menu avg=12
wait 2000
shot menu_again avg=12
diff menu menu_again as=same_state
stats menu
stats menu in=glow
stats menu in=far

# The glow must still be there. Measured: glow rgb[16.0, 15.2, 18.2] and luma 15.6
# against far rgb[10.6, 10.5, 15.3] and luma 10.8 - a localised lift whose warm bias
# (R and G gaining more than B) matches the emitter's own r=1 g=0.55 b=0.15.
assert menu@glow.luma > menu@far.luma * 1.3
# ...and it must be a GRADIENT, not a uniformly brighter corner: the far field is
# essentially flat (std 0.39) while the glow corner has real structure (std 25.1).
# A flat `unlit` backdrop would fail this even if someone raised its brightness.
assert menu@glow.std > menu@far.std * 3
